WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.880
We have to read a lot in
order to get better at the

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:07.050
language that we're learning.

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:08.880
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here.

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:14.730
Today, and I'm going to talk, this is
the second in my series of videos about

00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:21.030
my hierarchy of goals, the main goals
that I have when I learn a language, and

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:22.470
today I'm going to talk about reading.

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:26.820
Remember, if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe, click

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:28.230
on the bell for notifications.

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:32.265
If you follow on a podcast
service, please leave a review.

00:00:32.985 --> 00:00:40.065
So, uh, the first sort of goal that the,
you know, I've worked towards in learning

00:00:40.065 --> 00:00:43.935
languages is to acquire words and bear
in mind that all these different goals

00:00:43.935 --> 00:00:48.075
are very much interrelated, but these
are sort of the goals that I pursue.

00:00:48.705 --> 00:00:51.495
The second goal is
reading and I'll explain.

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:56.400
In order to learn words, we,
first of all, have to read them.

00:00:56.790 --> 00:00:57.360
It's a lot...

00:00:57.390 --> 00:01:00.510
I don't say we have to, we can also
hear them and eventually learn them,

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:05.820
but it's an awful lot easier in my
experience if I can also read them.

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:09.480
If I just hear them, it's
difficult for me to remember them.

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:11.850
Reading gives them form.

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:15.210
Uh, it's easier for me
to remember the word.

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:20.370
I identify the written word
with the word that I hear.

00:01:21.164 --> 00:01:24.074
Um, reading is easier than listening.

00:01:24.104 --> 00:01:27.914
I can work my way through a text
at LingQ, look up every word.

00:01:28.155 --> 00:01:31.604
Let's say I'm in sentence mode, it's
a brand new language and if there's

00:01:31.604 --> 00:01:36.134
seven words in that sentence, I can
look up each word and I can kind of

00:01:36.134 --> 00:01:38.414
figure out what the sentence means.

00:01:39.074 --> 00:01:44.774
Fuzzy, maybe not totally clear, but I
have a sense of what that sentence means.

00:01:44.835 --> 00:01:48.509
I have a sense of which words,
you know, correspond to the

00:01:48.509 --> 00:01:50.310
verb, to the noun and so forth.

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:51.990
So the reading is easy.

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:55.199
If I just heard, heard those words,
I wouldn't be able to do that.

00:01:55.800 --> 00:02:00.990
So reading is a sort of a step towards
being able to understand what you hear.

00:02:01.949 --> 00:02:07.440
This is interesting because of course,
historically, and from an evolutionary

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:13.450
perspective, we weren't, as, as human
beings, we weren't designed to read.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:18.585
Uh, for the vast majority of our
existence, even as you know, our

00:02:19.035 --> 00:02:24.285
ancestors, uh, you know, in the
evolutionary chain and even as human

00:02:24.285 --> 00:02:29.805
beings, it's only in the last 5,000
years that writing has existed.

00:02:30.645 --> 00:02:35.655
And for most of that period, a very
small percentage of the population

00:02:35.685 --> 00:02:38.115
read, books were very expensive.

00:02:38.115 --> 00:02:39.705
It was very expensive to print.

00:02:40.155 --> 00:02:44.835
This became, you know, it became
more widespread with the development

00:02:44.835 --> 00:02:47.775
of different methods of printing
initially in China and then

00:02:48.795 --> 00:02:52.035
in Europe with, um, Gutenberg.

00:02:52.035 --> 00:02:52.245
And so...

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:57.540
but, and, but even after that, it was
a small percentage of people who read.

00:02:57.570 --> 00:03:03.780
So for the human brain to be able to
decipher, to convert sort of written

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:11.250
lines or, uh, drawings, uh, in a text
to convert that into meaning and also

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:14.130
into sound is an amazing achievement.

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:19.365
Uh, and so for us, when we learn to
write even in our own language, that's

00:03:19.365 --> 00:03:27.195
a major, um, process, uh, in terms of
getting our brains to adapt to something

00:03:27.195 --> 00:03:28.275
that they weren't used to doing.

00:03:28.455 --> 00:03:30.285
And many people have difficulty reading.

00:03:30.945 --> 00:03:37.335
Uh, now, to make reading easier I've
always felt that it's important to

00:03:37.335 --> 00:03:41.655
combine the audio with the reading so
that, you know, it's like, if you're

00:03:41.655 --> 00:03:47.595
playing um, a musical instrument if
you have heard the tune, it's easier

00:03:47.895 --> 00:03:51.525
to play that tune on the piano or
on the guitar, whatever it might be,

00:03:51.525 --> 00:03:53.085
because you have an idea of what it is.

00:03:53.085 --> 00:03:55.125
You're trying to, what sort of
music you're trying to make.

00:03:55.425 --> 00:03:59.489
Similarly with reading if you can
hear what it is you're trying to

00:03:59.489 --> 00:04:03.030
read, you have a little bit of
momentum going into the reading.

00:04:03.209 --> 00:04:06.869
So I always combine audio with text.

00:04:06.899 --> 00:04:11.760
I, I certainly in an early stage
in my learning, I want to be able

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:17.640
to do both listen and read, but I
know that in order to get good at

00:04:17.669 --> 00:04:19.950
the language, I have to read a lot.

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:26.460
And if I look at my statistics in LingQ
uh, even in Arabic and Persian, which

00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:31.710
are the two most recent languages, I've
read about 500,000 words in each of them,

00:04:32.219 --> 00:04:37.020
and LingQ measures uh, not only when you
read something for the first time, but

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:41.640
also when you read it for a second and
the third time, which I do, and I'll get

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:43.380
into this sort of strategy of reading.

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:48.750
But even when I feel that I'm struggling
to make progress, I know that every

00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:53.070
time I'm doing either something new,
which therefore would have a lot of

00:04:53.070 --> 00:04:57.150
unknown words that I'm looking up, you
know, blue words, or if I'm rereading

00:04:57.150 --> 00:05:00.929
something that I've read before, because
there's still lots of words that are

00:05:00.929 --> 00:05:04.440
now yellow words that I have looked
up, but I still don't know those words.

00:05:04.890 --> 00:05:08.599
And it's just a process of reading
again, reading something that I've read.

00:05:09.345 --> 00:05:11.115
I'm consuming those words.

00:05:11.295 --> 00:05:16.065
My brain is slowly, slowly getting
used to reading in that language.

00:05:16.875 --> 00:05:20.415
Uh, this is particularly true if
the writing system is different.

00:05:20.835 --> 00:05:24.795
And, uh, even though say the
Cyrillic alphabet, which is used for

00:05:24.795 --> 00:05:30.765
Russian and Ukrainian, Tajik other
languages, um, is not that different

00:05:30.765 --> 00:05:33.555
from our, uh, Latin alphabet.

00:05:33.855 --> 00:05:42.555
But if I'm reading in Arabic or Persian,
Or Korean or Japanese or Chinese, every

00:05:42.555 --> 00:05:46.215
time I'm reading, I'm helping the brain
get used to this new writing system.

00:05:46.455 --> 00:05:52.050
Even if I know theoretically what
the letters represent, I continue

00:05:52.050 --> 00:05:56.160
to confuse them because there I'm
still fighting to decipher them.

00:05:56.700 --> 00:06:00.720
Whereas if I'm reading them in my own
language or my own writing system,

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:05.100
it's instant, the, the, you know, the
meaning of those letters is, is instant.

00:06:05.580 --> 00:06:11.280
Um, and, and I think this might be
similar to, you know, the situation

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:13.170
of people who struggle with dyslexia.

00:06:13.170 --> 00:06:17.670
And so they're fighting to decipher
each word whereas a fluent reader just

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:21.300
immediately converts the word to meaning.

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:24.719
Uh, when we're reading in a foreign
language, I find that we're always

00:06:24.719 --> 00:06:28.890
sub vocalizing, so that the sound
of the word is very important to us.

00:06:29.310 --> 00:06:34.289
Uh, when we read it in our own language,
um, I think it's just an instant meaning.

00:06:34.590 --> 00:06:35.520
How did we get that way?

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:38.370
Because ever since childhood,
we've been reading in that

00:06:38.370 --> 00:06:41.130
writing system in that language.

00:06:41.159 --> 00:06:44.260
And so it's just, it's just a matter
of the brain is so used to it.

00:06:44.610 --> 00:06:51.135
So therefore we have to read a
lot in order to get better at

00:06:51.135 --> 00:06:52.485
the language that we're learning.

00:06:52.485 --> 00:06:57.465
So if I say I'm at 500,000 words in
Arabic or Persian, I had a million

00:06:57.465 --> 00:07:01.255
and a half words read at least
according to statistics on LingQ in

00:07:01.255 --> 00:07:05.205
languages, like say Czech or Russian,
this doesn't account for the reading

00:07:05.205 --> 00:07:07.335
that I do away from the computer.

00:07:07.515 --> 00:07:13.425
Again, I read on my iPad or in LingQ in
order to look up words because I, I find

00:07:13.425 --> 00:07:18.075
it frustrating to be reading, uh, and, and
having a lot of words that I don't know.

00:07:18.495 --> 00:07:22.515
But once I get good enough in a
language, I like to read on paper because

00:07:22.515 --> 00:07:24.375
that's, you know, you have no help.

00:07:24.375 --> 00:07:26.805
You have, there's no audio,
there's no text to speech.

00:07:27.045 --> 00:07:28.125
You can't look up words.

00:07:28.125 --> 00:07:30.635
You just have to fight your
way through this text on paper.

00:07:31.115 --> 00:07:36.285
And I've always felt that when I am
first able to read a whole book from

00:07:36.285 --> 00:07:41.655
cover to cover paper book, conventional
book in a language, that's a major

00:07:41.655 --> 00:07:43.305
achievement, a major milestone.

00:07:43.605 --> 00:07:45.345
I'm not there in Arabic and Persian.

00:07:45.585 --> 00:07:49.395
There are just far too many words that
I don't know, but I continue reading.

00:07:49.425 --> 00:07:53.775
That's probably, you know, every day
I read some and either I'm looking up

00:07:53.775 --> 00:07:58.485
words because it's, you know, there's
a lot of blue words, unknown words, or

00:07:58.485 --> 00:08:00.165
it's material that I've read before.

00:08:00.945 --> 00:08:04.125
Perhaps moving some of the
yellow words along and status

00:08:04.125 --> 00:08:05.505
moving them perhaps to known.

00:08:05.895 --> 00:08:10.604
So I spend a lot of time reading and,
you know, because it's so often the

00:08:10.604 --> 00:08:14.505
case in language learning once we
get past that first stage where we

00:08:14.505 --> 00:08:17.474
suddenly discover this language and
we understand a few things and we can

00:08:17.474 --> 00:08:21.435
say a few things and we're very happy,
then we hit that long period where

00:08:21.435 --> 00:08:26.775
we're slowly acquiring these, you know,
lower frequency words, which we know.

00:08:27.570 --> 00:08:32.189
So during that period, it's very important
to have a sort of a reinforcement to

00:08:32.280 --> 00:08:37.140
convince yourself that you're actually
doing something and I'm getting somewhere.

00:08:37.140 --> 00:08:40.890
And, and I've always felt that,
you know, we need measures of

00:08:40.890 --> 00:08:45.120
our activity level because if we
are active, we will be learning.

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:48.840
And in that regard to the number
of words, read, which we track at

00:08:48.840 --> 00:08:52.680
LingQ, uh, even the number of words,
you know, that you have looked up.

00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:53.760
In other words, the LingQs create.

00:08:54.780 --> 00:08:57.840
These are all indicators
of how active you are.

00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:01.870
And so the reading is a
big activity at LingQ.

00:09:02.010 --> 00:09:06.930
It's one that's helping your brain get
used to the language and it's a measure

00:09:06.930 --> 00:09:09.510
of how active you are with the language.

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:15.450
And, uh, I will get onto the other
goals like listening, uh, which

00:09:15.450 --> 00:09:18.330
is easier to do because I can do
it while I'm doing other tasks.

00:09:18.570 --> 00:09:23.775
But reading does require actually being
in front of either be reading from

00:09:23.775 --> 00:09:27.435
the book or you're reading on the iPad
or on the computer or using LingQ, or

00:09:27.435 --> 00:09:30.314
you're using some other method to read.

00:09:30.495 --> 00:09:31.845
It's something that is dedicated.

00:09:31.845 --> 00:09:33.825
You cannot be reading and
driving at the same time.

00:09:33.855 --> 00:09:34.905
I don't recommend that.

00:09:35.444 --> 00:09:42.915
Uh, so, but reading, uh, you know, it's,
it's it's, if we look at reading aside

00:09:42.915 --> 00:09:50.520
from language, Illiteracy, um, people
who read well by and large do better.

00:09:50.730 --> 00:09:54.570
That's not to say that people who
don't read well are bad people, many

00:09:54.750 --> 00:09:58.860
people who don't read well are very
successful in a variety of fields.

00:09:59.930 --> 00:10:03.950
Over all people who read well do
better, you know, academically,

00:10:03.950 --> 00:10:06.110
professionally, socially, and so forth.

00:10:06.110 --> 00:10:10.610
So reading is very important and
people who read well, read a lot

00:10:11.250 --> 00:10:12.890
and the same with language learning.

00:10:12.890 --> 00:10:17.360
If you want to learn the language
well, you need to read a lot.

00:10:17.840 --> 00:10:23.990
And the reading can be either rereading,
you know, easy material, which I

00:10:23.990 --> 00:10:27.440
still find myself doing both, you
know, uh, some of the learner books

00:10:27.440 --> 00:10:28.460
in Persian, I still read through.

00:10:29.204 --> 00:10:34.875
Because to reinforce certain structures
of the language and a, and I'll read

00:10:34.875 --> 00:10:38.535
the mini stories again, uh, because
there's still words there that I still

00:10:38.535 --> 00:10:43.365
can't remember, but I'm, I vary that
with, you know, reading new material

00:10:43.365 --> 00:10:44.985
that has more blue words in it.

00:10:45.405 --> 00:10:50.175
And of course the, the feeling we have
when we see that the pages, even if

00:10:50.175 --> 00:10:55.095
I, if I bring in a newspaper article
in Persian and I see that the pages

00:10:55.860 --> 00:10:58.500
there's fewer and fewer blue words.

00:10:58.890 --> 00:11:02.070
Uh, there are more and more
white words that are known.

00:11:02.340 --> 00:11:05.290
So you have that sense of the,
the pages on LingQ becoming

00:11:05.310 --> 00:11:06.630
lighter and lighter in color.

00:11:06.900 --> 00:11:10.050
And that's an indication that
you are getting somewhere, even

00:11:10.050 --> 00:11:14.070
though at times you may struggle to
understand you may struggle to speak.

00:11:14.310 --> 00:11:20.010
So I guess my message is that, that
in my hierarchy of goals, if the first

00:11:20.010 --> 00:11:26.145
goal is to acquire more words, Uh, the
main way of acquiring that, those new

00:11:26.145 --> 00:11:31.454
words, passive vocabulary is reading,
reading in combination with listening,

00:11:31.454 --> 00:11:33.344
but I can't do it without the reading.

00:11:33.885 --> 00:11:37.744
And the amount of reading we
do on LingQ is easily measured.

00:11:38.185 --> 00:11:41.925
So it's, it's sort of a
measure of how active you are.

00:11:42.015 --> 00:11:45.825
It's an indication that you are
moving forward in the language, even

00:11:45.825 --> 00:11:47.685
though sometimes you think you aren't.

00:11:48.075 --> 00:11:51.765
And even if I'm reading something,
that's relatively uninteresting.

00:11:51.765 --> 00:11:53.265
Something that I've read before.

00:11:53.685 --> 00:11:58.755
I know from personal experience that just
being exposed to this, to the writing

00:11:58.755 --> 00:12:02.655
system, being exposed to this need to
read and to decipher what's written there.

00:12:04.440 --> 00:12:05.280
And that's helping me.

00:12:05.310 --> 00:12:07.380
So, I mean, we will do things.

00:12:07.380 --> 00:12:13.020
I regularly pull down a kettle 20
minute, 25 minute kettlebell routine

00:12:13.410 --> 00:12:17.070
from YouTube that I've done before, but
I know that it's good for me to do it.

00:12:17.070 --> 00:12:18.540
So I'm, I'm content to do it.

00:12:19.050 --> 00:12:24.090
Uh, the same with reading material
that I've read before and our system

00:12:24.090 --> 00:12:25.270
at LingQ does keep track of that.

00:12:25.665 --> 00:12:30.334
But at the same time, I'm motivated,
motivated to read new material where I'll

00:12:30.334 --> 00:12:34.814
acquire new words and where I'm learning
about things that are of interest to me.

00:12:34.814 --> 00:12:36.855
So you can do both kinds of reading.

00:12:36.855 --> 00:12:42.165
You can be reading specifically to learn
sort of a deliberate learning activity,

00:12:42.405 --> 00:12:46.095
or you can be reading to acquire more
information, to learn about different

00:12:46.095 --> 00:12:47.715
things that are of interest to you.

00:12:48.165 --> 00:12:52.215
But again, reading, uh,
whether you do it a digit...

00:12:52.245 --> 00:12:55.805
you know, with e-text or whether
you do it in a paper book is is a

00:12:55.805 --> 00:12:58.635
fundamental part of language learning.

00:12:58.815 --> 00:13:02.775
Now that's not to say that there
aren't lots of people who learn and

00:13:02.775 --> 00:13:08.145
don't read, and I am sure there are,
we read about the, you know, the

00:13:08.155 --> 00:13:12.495
Amazon jungle, where there's so many
different languages and people managed

00:13:12.495 --> 00:13:13.935
to learn each other's languages.

00:13:14.955 --> 00:13:20.385
I gather in Ethiopia, which is not a
country with a high rate of literacy uh,

00:13:20.415 --> 00:13:23.865
people nevertheless are quite multilingual
because there are so many languages

00:13:23.865 --> 00:13:25.545
there, so you can learn without reading.

00:13:25.605 --> 00:13:28.245
However, it's easier with reading.

00:13:28.605 --> 00:13:31.875
If you live surrounded by speakers
of other languages, you'll pick up

00:13:31.875 --> 00:13:35.925
those languages quite comfortably, but
most of us aren't in that situation.

00:13:36.165 --> 00:13:41.025
And so reading, which is a, it's
like a recording, but it's a written

00:13:41.055 --> 00:13:44.025
record of what someone's said.

00:13:44.835 --> 00:13:45.915
Uh, better.

00:13:45.960 --> 00:13:49.560
If you can hear what's there, because
that gives you momentum into the

00:13:49.560 --> 00:13:54.000
reading, you actually can hear the
words that you're reading, but to have

00:13:54.000 --> 00:13:58.260
the ability to read in combination
with listening is very, very powerful.

00:13:58.439 --> 00:14:02.550
So, uh, and in my mind, you know,
in terms of the statistics that I

00:14:02.550 --> 00:14:09.495
follow, uh, at LingQ of my reading
comes right after, um, my vocabulary.

00:14:09.615 --> 00:14:11.325
So I hope that was helpful.

00:14:11.625 --> 00:14:17.805
And I will continue in this series
about my goals when I learn languages.

00:14:18.135 --> 00:14:19.125
Thank you for listening.

00:14:19.155 --> 00:14:19.575
Bye for now.

